# Dedup Is Clause Level Both Ways

Hook corrections (2026-07-28, not owner rulings) during a rules-document dedup:

1. A bullet deleted as duplicate carried one extra list item the owner document lacked.
2. Kept bullets retained "anchor" clauses that restate the owner document's rule for the same scope, and the final report claimed the dedup complete anyway.

**Why:** Rules differ at clause granularity while editing happens at bullet granularity. Deleting whole bullets drops rulings; keeping whole bullets preserves duplicates. Overclaiming ("deleted every duplicate") while a verifier ledger lists residuals is the worse half of the failure.

**How to apply:** For every mixed bullet, test each clause, list item, and example against the owner document: same rule for the same scope is excised, unique clauses survive as a standalone line, and rules that merely share a theme across different surfaces stay whole. A single list item counts as content, so keep a trimmed line for anything the owner document lacks. Rewrite the surviving line so it stands without the duplicated anchor. Report deliberate retentions explicitly instead of claiming zero overlap. Afterward run a fresh three-way check (original, edited, owner document) asking what appears in neither, and reverify with a fresh reviewer after the fix round.
